        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d467ce0e-eecb-550e-8fb4-23c39e556dc4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin, Ian Sexton, Giuseppe Verdi, Maurice Duruflé\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens, Messa da Requiem, Requiem (Opus 9)\n- Organizations: Cór Cois Abhann\n- Concepts: Kipchaks, Cumans, Rus' people\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor, and the Polovtsian Dances form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of that opera.\n- Alexander Borodin's Polovtsian Dances are also called the Polovetsian Dances, and the Russian name refers to the Polovtsy, the term used by the Rus' people for the Kipchaks and Cumans.\n- In 1890, the melody later used for Stranger in Paradise was taken from Alexander Borodin's Gliding Dance of the Maidens from the Polovtsian Dances in Prince Igor.\n- Cór Cois Abhann is currently directed by Ian Sexton.\n- Cór Cois Abhann has performed large-scale works including Giuseppe Verdi's Messa da Requiem, Alexander Borodin's Polovtsian Dances, and Maurice Duruflé's Requiem (Opus 9).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "221c6439-c871-5b92-83a4-cae3bf5f2032",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".\nPage: Cór Cois Abhann. Sentence: The choir is currently directed by Ian Sexton and has performed large scale works including Verdi's \"Messa da Requiem, Borodin's \"Polovtsian Dances\" and Duruflé's \"Requiem (Opus 9)\".",
